assertion  XSH.ldexp.001
anchor     functions/ldexp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/ldexp.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       ldexp(1.5, 3), (1, -2), (-3, 2), (0.5, 1), (3, 0), (0.75, -4) and
           (2.5, 5) give 12, 0.25, -12, 1, 3, 0.046875 and 80.
test       t/001-ldexp-values.c

assertion  XSH.ldexp.002
anchor     functions/ldexp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/ldexp.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     functions/ldexp.html#ERRORS
class      required
since      1988
says       ldexp(1, INT_MAX), ldexp(-1, INT_MAX) and ldexp(MAX, 1), with MAX the
           largest finite value of the type, overflow: the result is HUGE_VAL
           (negated for a negative x) and the range error is reported through
           errno (ERANGE) and the overflow exception as math_errhandling selects.
test       t/002-ldexp-overflow.c

assertion  XSH.ldexp.003
anchor     functions/ldexp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/ldexp.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       ldexp(1, INT_MIN) and ldexp(-1, INT_MIN) return a zero (the range
           error that may accompany it is not judged).
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/003-ldexp-underflow-zero.c

assertion  XSH.ldexp.004
anchor     functions/ldexp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/ldexp.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       A NaN argument gives a NaN result.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/004-ldexp-nan.c

assertion  XSH.ldexp.005
anchor     functions/ldexp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/ldexp.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       ldexp(+0, 5), (-0, 5), (+Inf, -3) and (-Inf, -3) return the first
           argument, with its sign.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/005-ldexp-zero-inf.c

assertion  XSH.ldexp.006
anchor     functions/ldexp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/ldexp.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       ldexp(x, 0) returns x for x equal to 3, -3, 0.1 and -0.1.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/006-ldexp-exponent-zero.c

assertion  XSH.ldexp.007
anchor     functions/ldexp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/ldexp.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       The smallest normal value of the type scaled by 2^-1 gives half of it,
           exactly, when subnormal numbers exist.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard). A range error may accompany the result; it is not judged.
test       t/007-ldexp-subnormal.c

assertion  XSH.ldexp.008
anchor     functions/ldexp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/ldexp.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.23
class      required
since      1988
says       With errno holding EDOM before the call, ldexp(1.5, 3) leaves it
           unchanged and, when exception flags are in use, raises none of the
           invalid, divide-by-zero and overflow exceptions.
test       t/008-ldexp-no-error.c
